<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Launch background.

      Pure black, matching the intro clip's own background exactly. On a fresh
      launch the user sees the intro first, so this colour is what sits behind
      the Android launch window before React Native has drawn anything. Matching
      it means the hand-off from the native launch window to the first JS frame
      is invisible instead of a flicker.

      Why this exists at all: without an explicit windowBackground, this theme
      inherits `?android:attr/colorBackground` through Theme.AppCompat.DayNight,
      which follows the system light/dark setting. On a device in light mode
      that is *white* — so the app used to open with a white flash, and on a
      dark-mode device with an unbranded near-black panel. Either way the window
      said nothing, and on a slow cold start it lingered for seconds. Pinning it
      removes both problems and cannot be wrong regardless of system theme.

      Home uses #050506 (near-black, to avoid OLED smear), but black is correct
      here: this background is only visible on a launch that is about to show
      the intro, whose own background is #000000.
    -->
    <color name="sanket_launch_background">#FF000000</color>
</resources>
